  "blocks": [
    {
      "block_id": 0,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "For another example, an LF control byte of $80 (= 1000 0000 binary) turns on bits only for those \npoints of the D destination rectangle where the corresponding bits of A, B, and C sources were all \non (ABC = 1, bit 7 of LF on). All other points in the rectangle, which correspond to other combi­\nnations for A, B, and C, will be 0. This is because bits 6 through 0 of the LF control byte, which \nspecify the D output for these situations, are set to 0.\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.09700012207031,
        55.96501922607422,
        517.361083984375,
        118.05504608154297
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.141632404676189,
        0.07250481794067315,
        0.861077291860757,
        0.15294481698546677
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 1,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "DESIGNING THE LF CONTROL BYTE WITH MINTERMS\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.5469970703125,
        147.40203857421875,
        378.9063720703125,
        157.90203857421875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1423813635088748,
        0.1909649655928028,
        0.6306382192846627,
        0.2045681162555721
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 2,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "One approach to designing the LF control byte uses logic equations. Each of the rows in the truth \ntable corresponds to a “ mintenn” , which is a particular assignment of values to the A, B, and C \nbits. For instance, the first minterm is usually written ABC, or “ not A and not B and not C” . \nThe last is written as ABC.\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.09700012207031,
        167.2010498046875,
        517.3613891601562,
        216.947021484375
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.141632404676189,
        0.21661534013965306,
        0.8610777997844169,
        0.28106314467533344
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 3,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "NOTE\n",
      "bbox": [
        284.47698974609375,
        231.99603271484375,
        314.5804443359375,
        242.49603271484375
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.47347333131586056,
        0.3005597129699785,
        0.5235764448980541,
        0.3141628636327478
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 4,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Two terms that are adjacent are and’ed, and two terms that are separated by “ +” are \nor’ed. “ And” has a higher precedence, so AB + BC is equal to (AB) + (BC).\n",
      "bbox": [
        109.7869873046875,
        251.09503173828125,
        492.553955078125,
        274.5420227050781
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.18272553664420324,
        0.3253031949913027,
        0.8197891933958953,
        0.3556796664874864
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 5,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Any function can be written as a sum of minterms. If we wanted to calculate the function where \nD Js one when the A bit is set and the C bit is clear, or when the B bit is set, we can write that as \nAC+B, or “ A and notC or B” . Since “ 1 and A” is “ A” :\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.7869873046875,
        289.6910095214844,
        517.7673950195312,
        326.38800048828125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14278079467500962,
        0.37530575696861784,
        0.86175354142153,
        0.42284810906305803
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 6,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D=AC+B\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.4859848022461,
        341.82159423828125,
        151.3059844970703,
        351.82159423828125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558091635320157,
        0.4428429186867937,
        0.2518282712137618,
        0.4557983002703835
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 7,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D = A(1)C + (l)B(l)\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.4859848022461,
        368.43701171875,
        189.74403381347656,
        378.93701171875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558091635320157,
        0.4773242076333956,
        0.31580318628638626,
        0.4909273582961649
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 8,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Since either A or A is true (1 = A + A), and similarly for B, and C; we can expand the above \nequation further:\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.78998565673828,
        394.83599853515625,
        517.2645263671875,
        418.1839904785156
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.142785785024968,
        0.5115251023960655,
        0.8609165848147688,
        0.5417733168797457
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 9,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D = A(1)C + (l)B(l)\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.48898315429688,
        434.1839904785156,
        189.988525390625,
        444.6839904785156
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558590670315998,
        0.5625019274134894,
        0.3162101093265044,
        0.5761050780762587
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 10,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D = A(B + B)C + (A + A)B(C + C)\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.48898315429688,
        460.6839904785156,
        254.56344604492188,
        471.1839904785156
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558590670315998,
        0.5968336886100024,
        0.4236862986272144,
        0.6104368392727717
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D = ABC + ABC + AB(C + C) + AB(C + C)\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.48898315429688,
        486.63299560546875,
        296.4898986816406,
        497.13299560546875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558590670315998,
        0.6304516149234229,
        0.49346718747127055,
        0.6440547655861922
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 12,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "D = ABC + ABC + ABC + ABC + ABC + ABC\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.68898010253906,
        512.781982421875,
        312.6394348144531,
        523.281982421875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16591877447366665,
        0.6643286251465031,
        0.5203458980440774,
        0.6779317758092724
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 13,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "After eliminating duplicates, we end up with the five minterms:\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.78997802734375,
        538.6809692382812,
        362.38116455078125,
        549.1809692382812
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1427857723268765,
        0.6978817508299937,
        0.6031342546865355,
        0.711484901492763
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 14,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "AC+B = ABC + ABC + ABC + ABC + ABC\n",
      "bbox": [
        99.48897552490234,
        565.0800170898438,
        299.02044677734375,
        575.5800170898438
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.16558589400506846,
        0.7320827246660375,
        0.4976789412514162,
        0.7456858753288068
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 15,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "These correspond to BLTCONO bit positions of 6, 4, 7, 3, and 2, according to our truth table, \nwhich we would then set, and clear the rest.\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.78997802734375,
        590.8789672851562,
        517.2665405273438,
        614.22900390625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1427857723268765,
        0.7655062490896678,
        0.8609199371109242,
        0.7957571125313744
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 16,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The wide range of logic operations allow some sophisticated graphics techniques. For instance, \nyou can move the image of a car across some pre-existing building images with a few blits. Pro­\nducing this effect requires predrawn images of the car, the buildings (or background), and a car\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.03997802734375,
        629.1780395507812,
        517.3905029296875,
        665.3770141601562
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1432018633890556,
        0.8151241586395331,
        0.8611262557015701,
        0.8620213115394463
      ]
    },
